.TH CONDUITSCOPE 1 "2026" "conduitscope 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
conduitscope \- decode Modbus/TCP, DNP3, IEC 60870-5-104, S7comm/COTP, S7comm-Plus (Siemens TIA Portal / S7-1200/1500), IEC 61850 MMS (Manufacturing Message Specification, ISO 9506), EtherNet/IP (CIP explicit and implicit messaging), PROFINET RT (DCP and cyclic real-time IO), IEC 61850-8-1 GOOSE, IEC 61850-9-2 Sampled Values, EtherCAT, BACnet/IP, HART-IP, OPC UA Binary, and MQTT (v3.1/v3.1.1/v5.0, including Sparkplug B) traffic from offline pcap/pcapng captures
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B conduitscope
[\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-color\fR|\fB\-\-color\fR] [\fB\-\-log\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B conduitscope
reads a pcap or pcapng capture file, or optionally a live network
interface (see \fBLIVE CAPTURE\fR below), and decodes Modbus/TCP, DNP3,
IEC 60870-5-104 (TCP port 2404), S7comm (Siemens S7 PLC protocol, over
TPKT/COTP, TCP port 102), S7comm-Plus (Siemens TIA Portal / S7-1200/1500's
newer, object-oriented protocol, the same TPKT/COTP transport and TCP port
102 S7comm uses but disambiguated by its own protocol id byte, 0x72 vs
S7comm's 0x32 -- GetMultiVariables/SetMultiVariables/SetVariable/
DeleteObject fully decoded, everything else named but not body-decoded),
IEC 61850 MMS (Manufacturing Message
Specification, ISO 9506, the same TPKT/COTP transport and TCP port 102
S7comm uses, but a distinct, independent application protocol with its own
Session/Presentation/ACSE layering -- detected by its own separate
structural gate, tried once S7comm's own gate has already failed), and
EtherNet/IP (CIP explicit messaging, TCP port
44818) traffic found inside its TCP streams, plus EtherNet/IP CIP I/O
(implicit messaging, UDP port 2222) traffic found inside its UDP streams,
plus PROFINET RT (EtherType 0x8892, DCP device discovery/configuration and
cyclic real-time IO), IEC 61850-8-1 GOOSE (EtherType 0x88B8, ASN.1
BER-encoded GOOSE PDU), IEC 61850-9-2 Sampled Values (EtherType 0x88BA,
GOOSE's sibling protocol, ASN.1 BER-encoded SavPdu), and EtherCAT (EtherType
0x88A4, plain fixed-binary-layout framing, no ASN.1/BER) traffic found
directly on the wire, with no
IP/TCP/UDP layer at all, plus BACnet/IP (UDP port 47808/0xBAC0, ASHRAE 135
Annex J, detected by payload shape rather than port) traffic found inside
its UDP streams, plus HART-IP (UDP/TCP port 5094, IEC 62591 / HCF_SPEC-151,
tried last of all protocols on both transports due to its own genuinely
weak structural detection gate) traffic found on either, plus OPC UA Binary
(TCP port 4840, UA-TCP transport / OPC UA Secure Conversation, OPC 10000-6,
tried FIRST of all protocols due to its own strong structural detection
gate) traffic found inside its TCP streams, plus MQTT (v3.1/v3.1.1/v5.0,
conventionally TCP port 1883, tried LAST of all protocols \-\- even after
HART-IP \-\- due to having no fixed magic number or length field of its own)
and, layered on top of it, Sparkplug B (the Eclipse Tahu IIoT convention,
protobuf-encoded payloads under the \fIspBv1.0/...\fR topic namespace,
decoded via a hand-rolled Protocol Buffers reader) traffic found inside its
TCP streams, and checks
decoded traffic against a
zone-and-conduit segmentation policy (IEC 62443 / NIS2 style work) via
\fBpolicy validate\fR.
.PP
Both classic pcap (what tcpdump writes by default) and pcapng (what
\fBdumpcap\fR/current Wireshark default to) are read transparently --
auto-detected from the file itself, nothing to specify on the command
line. See \fBdocs/MANUAL.md\fR's "pcap vs. pcapng" section for exactly
which pcapng block types are understood.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B decode (\fB\-r\fR \fIFILE\fR | \fB\-i\fR \fIINTERFACE\fR) [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
Decode a capture (or live interface) and print each recognized packet. See
\fBOPTIONS (decode)\fR below.
.TP
.B info \fB\-r\fR \fIFILE\fR
Print pcap file metadata and a protocol histogram. Offline files only; see
\fBLIVE CAPTURE\fR for the live equivalent.
.TP
.B interfaces
List network interfaces available for live capture. See \fBLIVE CAPTURE\fR
below.
.TP
.B policy validate (\fB\-r\fR \fIFILE\fR | \fB\-i\fR \fIINTERFACE\fR) \fB\-\-policy\fR \fIFILE\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
Zone/conduit policy check: decodes the capture (or live interface) exactly
as \fBdecode\fR would, groups it into TCP flows, and checks each against the
zones/conduits declared in the policy file (a restricted, dependency-free
YAML subset -- see \fBdocs/MANUAL.md\fR's POLICY FILE FORMAT section).
Prints a compliant/non-compliant report; see \fBOPTIONS (policy validate)\fR
and \fBEXIT STATUS\fR below.
.TP
.B version
Print version and build information, including whether this build has live
capture support.
.SH OPTIONS (decode)
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-read " " \fIFILE\fR
Input capture file (classic pcap or pcapng, auto-detected). Required unless \fB\-i\fR is given; mutually exclusive with it.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interface " " \fINAME\fR
Capture live from this network interface instead of reading a file. Required
unless \fB\-r\fR is given; mutually exclusive with it. Requires this build to
have libpcap/Npcap support -- see \fBLIVE CAPTURE\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-filter \fIBPF\fR
BPF capture filter (tcpdump syntax). Only meaningful with \fB\-i\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-duration \fISECONDS\fR
Stop a live capture (\fB\-i\fR) after this many seconds. Default: 0
(unlimited -- rely on \fB\-\-max\-packets\fR and/or Ctrl+C instead).
.TP
.B \-\-snaplen \fIBYTES\fR
Maximum bytes captured per packet with \fB\-i\fR. Default: 65535.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-promiscuous
With \fB\-i\fR, don't put the interface into promiscuous mode. Promiscuous
is the default (see \fBLIVE CAPTURE\fR for why).
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Write output here instead of stdout.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " " {text,json,csv}
Output format. Default: text.
.TP
.B \-\-protocol {auto,modbus,dnp3,iec104,s7comm,s7comm\-plus,mms,enip,profinet,goose,sv,ethercat,bacnet,hartip,opcua,mqtt}
Restrict decoding to one protocol. Default: auto (tries all ten TCP
protocols, independent of port number, plus CIP I/O and BACnet/IP on every
UDP payload,
plus PROFINET RT on every non-IPv4 Ethernet frame carrying EtherType
0x8892, plus GOOSE on every non-IPv4 Ethernet frame carrying EtherType
0x88B8, plus Sampled Values on every non-IPv4 Ethernet frame carrying
EtherType 0x88BA, plus EtherCAT on every non-IPv4 Ethernet frame carrying
EtherType 0x88A4; OPC UA is tried first of all, ahead of every other TCP
protocol, since its own 3-byte ASCII MessageType magic (HEL/ACK/ERR/RHE/
OPN/CLO/MSG) has zero collision risk against any other protocol's own
detection gate; EtherNet/IP is tried next, then IEC 104 before Modbus, since an
IEC 104 I-format APDU with sequence numbers 0/0 -- universal at session
start -- coincidentally also matches Modbus's MBAP-header detection,
S7comm\-Plus is tried on the same TPKT/COTP framing and TCP port 102 as
S7comm and MMS, disambiguated by its own protocol id byte (0x72 vs S7comm's
0x32, so no collision risk with either), and MMS is
tried immediately after (same TPKT/COTP framing, same TCP port
102, S7comm's own stronger single-byte protocol-id gate tried first) using
its own separate structural gate (a plausible Session SPDU type, a "bare
MMS" PDU tag, or "bare Presentation" bytes -- see \fBdocs/MANUAL.md\fR's
PROTOCOL COVERAGE MMS section), and
HART-IP is tried second-to-last, on both TCP and UDP, since its own 8-byte
header check is the weakest structural gate of any protocol here and is
known to collide with Modbus/TCP's own detection on TCP (never resolved by
reordering -- see
\fBdocs/MANUAL.md\fR's PROTOCOL DETECTION section), and MQTT is tried last
of all: it has no fixed magic number or length field of its own, so it only
gets a turn once every other protocol (HART-IP included) has already
declined the bytes; real validation nonetheless found two genuine
collisions this ordering alone didn't resolve -- a v5 CONNACK payload that
coincidentally satisfied Modbus/TCP's own \fIprotocol_id==0\fR tell (fixed
with a length-plausibility cap in Modbus's TCP parser) and small MQTT
packet identifiers that coincidentally satisfied HART-IP's own weak gate
(worked around on the test-fixture side, deliberately without weakening
HART-IP's own gate) -- see \fBdocs/MANUAL.md\fR's PROTOCOL DETECTION
section. \fBenip\fR covers both
EtherNet/IP explicit messaging (TCP) and CIP I/O implicit messaging (UDP).
\fBmms\fR is IEC 61850 MMS -- shares S7comm's exact transport/port but is a
distinct protocol; there is no separate \fB\-\-mms\-port\fR, see
\fB\-\-s7comm\-port\fR below.
\fBsv\fR is IEC 61850-9-2 Sampled Values. \fBethercat\fR is EtherCAT.
\fBbacnet\fR is BACnet/IP.
\fBhartip\fR is HART-IP, tried on both TCP and UDP.
\fBprofinet\fR covers both DCP and cyclic real-time IO.
\fBopcua\fR is OPC UA Binary (UA-TCP/Secure Conversation), TCP only.
.TP
.B \-\-modbus\-port \fIPORT\fR
Additional TCP port to treat as expected for Modbus. Repeatable. Does not
gate detection, only whether the port is flagged as unexpected in the output.
.TP
.B \-\-dnp3\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for DNP3. Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-iec104\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for IEC 60870-5-104. Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-s7comm\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for COTP/S7comm, MMS, and S7comm\-Plus
(repeatable) \-\- all three ride the identical TPKT/COTP transport and TCP
port 102, and there is no separate \fB\-\-mms\-port\fR or
\fB\-\-s7comm\-plus\-port\fR. Does not change detection, only whether the
port is flagged as unexpected.
.TP
.B \-\-enip\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for EtherNet/IP explicit messaging (TCP). Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-enip\-io\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for EtherNet/IP CIP I/O implicit messaging (UDP). Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-bacnet\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for BACnet/IP (UDP). Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-hartip\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for HART-IP. Repeatable. Applies to both TCP
and UDP, since HART-IP uses the same port number on either transport.
.TP
.B \-\-opcua\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for OPC UA. Repeatable. TCP only.
.TP
.B \-\-mqtt\-port \fIPORT\fR
Same as \fB\-\-modbus\-port\fR, for MQTT (built\-in default: 1883).
Repeatable. TCP only.
.TP
.B \-\-max\-packets \fIN\fR
Stop after decoding this many packets. Default: 0 (unlimited). With
\fB\-i\fR, also bounds a live capture.
.TP
.B \-\-stats
Print an aggregate summary instead of one line per packet. Ignores
\fB\-\-format\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Abort on the first malformed packet instead of warning and continuing.
.SH OPTIONS (policy validate)
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-read " " \fIFILE\fR
Input capture file (classic pcap or pcapng, auto-detected). Required unless \fB\-i\fR is given; mutually exclusive with it.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interface " " \fINAME\fR
Check live traffic from this network interface instead of reading a file.
Required unless \fB\-r\fR is given; mutually exclusive with it. No
\fB\-\-max\-packets\fR equivalent here -- use \fB\-\-duration\fR and/or Ctrl+C.
.TP
.B \-\-filter \fIBPF\fR
BPF capture filter (tcpdump syntax). Only meaningful with \fB\-i\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-duration \fISECONDS\fR
Stop a live capture (\fB\-i\fR) after this many seconds. Default: 0
(unlimited -- rely on Ctrl+C instead).
.TP
.B \-\-snaplen \fIBYTES\fR
Maximum bytes captured per packet with \fB\-i\fR. Default: 65535.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-promiscuous
Same meaning as \fBdecode\fR's \fB\-\-no\-promiscuous\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-policy \fIFILE\fR
Zone/conduit policy file. Required. See \fBdocs/MANUAL.md\fR's POLICY FILE
FORMAT section for the schema.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Write the report here instead of stdout.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " " {text,json}
Report format. Default: text.
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Abort on the first malformed packet instead of warning and continuing.
.SH LIVE CAPTURE
\fB\-i/\-\-interface\fR and \fBinterfaces\fR require this build to have been
compiled with libpcap (Linux) or the Npcap SDK (Windows) found by CMake --
an optional, build-time-detected dependency, the one exception to
conduitscope's otherwise zero-dependency design. A build without it is
otherwise unaffected; \fB\-i\fR/\fBinterfaces\fR just report that clearly at
runtime instead of not existing.
.PP
A live capture stops -- decoding/reporting whatever was captured so far,
same as reaching the end of an offline file -- on \fB\-\-duration\fR
elapsing, \fB\-\-max\-packets\fR being reached (\fBdecode\fR only), or
Ctrl+C (SIGINT), which is caught and used to stop cleanly rather than
killing the process outright.
.PP
Promiscuous mode is on by default (\fB\-\-no\-promiscuous\fR turns it off)
because the primary use case -- watching a mirrored/SPAN switch port for
zone/conduit traffic between two other devices -- needs to see traffic not
addressed to the capturing host at all.
.PP
On Windows, install the Npcap SDK to build with live-capture support, and
the separate Npcap runtime/driver on whatever machine actually runs a
conduitscope binary built with it -- these are two different installs. See
\fBdocs/MANUAL.md\fR's LIVE CAPTURE section for the full reference,
including why this has only been validated on Linux so far.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress non-essential diagnostic/warning output.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable ANSI color in \fBdecode\fR's text\-format output. Mutually exclusive
with \fB\-\-color\fR. See \fBCOLORIZED OUTPUT\fR below.
.TP
.B \-\-color
Force ANSI color in \fBdecode\fR's text\-format output, even when not
writing directly to a terminal (e.g. piping to a pager that understands
color, like \fBless \-R\fR). Mutually exclusive with \fB\-\-no\-color\fR.
See \fBCOLORIZED OUTPUT\fR below.
.TP
.B \-\-log\-file \fIFILE\fR
Write diagnostic/warning messages to \fIFILE\fR instead of stderr.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit. Available at every command level.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version, compiler, platform, and build type, then exit.
.SH COLORIZED OUTPUT
Applies to \fBdecode\fR's \fBtext\fR format only (not \fBjson\fR/\fBcsv\fR,
and not \fBpolicy validate\fR's report, which stays plain text by design --
it's meant to be diffed/archived/piped as an audit artifact). The
\fB[protocol]\fR tag is colored per protocol: cyan for Modbus, magenta for
DNP3, green for IEC 104, blue for S7comm/COTP, bold blue for MMS
(deliberately close to S7comm's plain blue \-\- they share the same
TPKT/COTP transport/port, bold distinguishes MMS at a glance), bold magenta
for S7comm\-Plus (same reasoning \-\- shares the identical transport/port
with S7comm and MMS, bold magenta keeps it visually distinct from both
while still grouped with the S7 family), yellow for
EtherNet/IP,
bright cyan for PROFINET RT, bright green for GOOSE, bright magenta for
Sampled Values, bright yellow for EtherCAT, bright blue for BACnet/IP,
bright white for HART-IP, bright red for OPC UA, bold cyan for MQTT
(deliberately close to Modbus's plain cyan, but bold and otherwise distinct
from every other tag color -- MQTT shares no transport/port with any other
decoded protocol), dim
for everything else
recognized but not OT\-specific. A Modbus exception response's summary, and a
\fBparse\-error\fR packet's whole line, are bold red. Notes are dim.
.PP
Color is used only when writing directly to an interactive terminal by
default \-\- never into a file via \fB\-o\fR, and never when piped. Force it
on with \fB\-\-color\fR (e.g. to pipe into \fBless \-R\fR) or off with
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR; the two are mutually exclusive.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. For \fBpolicy validate\fR: the capture is COMPLIANT.
.TP
.B 1
Fatal error (bad arguments, unreadable/unrecognized input file, a
\fB\-\-strict\fR parse failure, or, for \fBpolicy validate\fR, a policy file
that couldn't be opened or failed validation).
.TP
.B 2
Currently unused. Reserved rather than reused: an earlier release used this
for \fBpolicy validate\fR while it was still a documented stub.
.TP
.B 3
\fBpolicy validate\fR only: the capture and policy were both valid, but the
capture is NON-COMPLIANT (at least one violation and/or unclassified flow).
.SH LIMITATIONS
A handful of rare/obsolete pcapng block types (the pre-2000s "Packet Block",
Interface Statistics Blocks, Name Resolution Blocks, Decryption Secrets
Blocks, and custom/vendor block types) are skipped rather than decoded --
no mainstream capture tool writes these, so this essentially never matters
in practice; general TCP stream reassembly is implemented but
narrowly scoped: a Modbus PDU, a single DNP3 data-link frame's own
header/blocks, an IEC 104 APDU, an EtherNet/IP encapsulation message, or a
TPKT/COTP frame split across TCP segments IS
reassembled (per directional TCP flow, using each protocol's own declared
length); authoritative (transaction-ID + TCP-session, non-heuristic) Modbus
request/response pairing is layered on top of the always-on payload-shape
heuristic, but only pairs a response when its request is actually present
on the same TCP session in this capture; a genuine S7comm message chained
across multiple complete TPKT/COTP frames via COTP's own EOT bit is also
reassembled, though real-capture validation of that path so far only
confirms it is transparent on content-free "priming" fragments, not genuine
multi-frame content splitting (synthetic-fixture-validated only); IPv4
only; no IPv4 fragment reassembly; DNP3
header and per-block CRCs are not validated; a DNP3 application fragment
spanning more than one data-link frame IS reassembled across separate TCP
segments (per TCP flow), though this path has no real-capture validation
yet; DNP3 point values are decoded
only for the group/variation combinations in the built-in point-format
table (an object header outside it is still located and skipped by
computed length); DNP3 absolute timestamps are shown as a raw
milliseconds-since-epoch count, not a calendar date; IEC 104 needs no
cross-frame reassembly (one I-format APDU always carries exactly one ASDU)
but its ASDU type-ID decode table, like DNP3's, covers only a "first pass"
set of common monitoring/command/setpoint type IDs -- an ASDU outside that
set is still identified by type name and structurally located, just not
value-decoded; EtherNet/IP's CIP explicit-message value decoding covers a
"first pass" set of generic common/Connection-Manager services plus,
specifically when the request path addresses a Rockwell Logix5000 named
tag (not a class/instance path), the Symbol-object tag services -- a
class/instance-addressed request reusing one of those same service codes
is shown structurally instead, and NOP (command 0x0000) is deliberately
not recognized as a command at all (see \fBdocs/MANUAL.md\fR's LIMITATIONS
and PROTOCOL COVERAGE); EtherNet/IP CIP I/O (implicit messaging, UDP port
2222) is now decoded too, but only its Sequenced Address Item (connection
ID + sequence number) is value-decoded -- the Connected Data Item's actual
I/O data is shown only as raw hex, never value-decoded (no generic
self-describing type, and this decoder doesn't track a connection's
negotiated transport class), datagrams are decoded independently with no
cross-datagram correlation to the Forward_Open that established a
connection, and no real capture was found to validate this decoder against
(synthetic-fixture-validated only); PROFINET RT (EtherType 0x8892) is also
now decoded -- DCP (device discovery/configuration: full PDU header, block
list, five value-decoded block types) is validated against two real
captures, one of which caught a genuine decoding bug (a BlockInfo/
BlockQualifier 2-byte prefix on certain DCP blocks) before this feature
ever shipped; cyclic real-time IO datagrams are decoded too (IO data plus
the CycleCounter/DataStatus/TransferStatus trailer), but the IO data's
length is inferred, not read from a field (indistinguishable from
Ethernet minimum-frame padding on a short datagram), no real capture with
cyclic IO data was found to validate that path against, and a DCP Xid is
never used to authoritatively pair a request to its response; IEC 61850-8-1
GOOSE (EtherType 0x88B8) is also now decoded -- every IECGoosePdu field
(gocbRef/datSet/goID/timestamp/stNum/sqNum/confRev/simulation/ndsCom) and
every allData value type (booleans, bit-strings, integers, floating point,
strings, timestamps, and nested array/structure values, recursively) --
validated against four real captures totaling 494 real GOOSE frames, but
every one of those frames happens to share the same narrow shape (every
optional field present, allData values only ever boolean/bit-string), so
optional-field absence, every other allData type, a GSE Management PDU
(the outer APDU's other, undecoded shape), and a truncated/malformed frame
are all validated only against a hand-built synthetic fixture, not an
independent real capture; IEC 61850-9-2 Sampled Values (EtherType 0x88BA,
GOOSE's sibling protocol) is also now decoded -- every ASDU field
(svID/datSet/smpCnt/confRev/refrTm/smpSynch/smpRate/smpMod/gmidData) except
seqData, the sample payload itself, which is deliberately shown only as raw
hex and never value-decoded (no generic self-describing type, same
reasoning as CIP I/O's Connected Data Item above); unlike GOOSE, every ASDU
in a frame's seqASDU is decoded, not just the first; despite a genuine
multi-source search, no real Sampled Values capture was found anywhere, so
this decoder is validated only against a hand-built synthetic fixture,
covering every field, both enumerated fields' full value sets, multi-ASDU
framing, and every malformed/truncated path; EtherCAT (EtherType 0x88A4,
plain fixed-binary-layout, no ASN.1/BER) is also now decoded -- the 2-byte
frame header (Length/Reserved/Type) and, for Type 1 ("EtherCAT command")
frames, the chained EtherCAT datagram(s) (Cmd/Idx/Adp-Ado-or-logical-address/
Len+flags/Irq/Data/Working Counter) -- Data is deliberately never
value-decoded, same reasoning as SV's seqData above; its frame-header Type
field is a genuinely weaker structural detection signal than this tool's
other raw-Ethernet decoders (5 of 16 possible values, vs. GOOSE/SV's
1-in-256 outer tag), so the dedicated EtherType remains the primary
confidence source; validated against a genuine 986-frame real capture (zero
declared-Length-vs-actual-bytes mismatches across all 986 frames, confirming
the chain-bounding design), but APRW/FPRW/BRW/LRW/ARMW/FRMW/EXT/NOP, the
Circulating bit, VLAN tagging, and non-Type-1 frames are validated only
against a hand-built synthetic fixture; BACnet/IP (UDP port 47808/0xBAC0,
detected by payload shape, not port) is also now decoded -- BVLC framing
(including every BBMD/foreign-device-table management function), NPDU
(including DEST/SRC routing and Network Layer Messages, named only), and
APDU (all 8 PDU types, byte-accurate including segmentation) are decoded,
but service value-decoding is a deliberate "first pass" covering only
Who-Is/I-Am/Who-Has/I-Have device discovery and ReadProperty/
WriteProperty/generic-Error -- every other service is named only, shown as
raw hex, as is a constructed/array PropertyValue and a segmented APDU's
own service data (no cross-packet reassembly); validated against a genuine
54-frame real capture (Original-Unicast-NPDU ReadProperty traffic against
trend-log objects), but device discovery, WriteProperty, every
PropertyValue type besides a scalar Unsigned, every other BVLC function,
and NPDU routing fields are validated only against a hand-built synthetic
fixture; HART-IP (UDP/TCP port 5094, IEC 62591 / HCF_SPEC-151) is also now
decoded -- the 8-byte HART-IP header, all four MessageID-selected body
shapes (Session Initiate, Session Close, Keep Alive, Pass Through), and,
inside Pass-Through, the full wired-HART Data-Link PDU (Delimiter/Address/
Command/Byte-Count/Response-Code/Device-Status/Data/Checksum) -- but its
own structural detection gate (an 8-byte header with only loose value
checks) is the weakest of any protocol in this tool, and is a KNOWN,
ACCEPTED, DOCUMENTED LIMITATION: on TCP it collides with Modbus/TCP's own
MBAP-header detection on certain byte patterns (reordering the two was
tried and rejected -- it regressed ~2% of this tool's own Modbus/TCP test
corpus -- so HART-IP is deliberately tried last), and, confirmed on a
genuine real capture, the same weak gate also false-positives against
unrelated background TCP traffic (TLS, SMB) that has nothing to do with
either protocol; the Data-Link Checksum is surfaced raw and never verified;
the Device-Status byte's flags are decoded but the header's own Status byte
is, per the spec itself, always 0 in current usage; commands 31 and 203 are
decoded structurally but deliberately given no authoritative top-level name
(no reliable source names either, only an inferred pairing); command
value-decoding is a "first pass" covering the most common read/write/
identification commands, with commands 77 and 178 in particular named but
not value-decoded; and validation is against both a comprehensive synthetic
fixture and a genuine 116-frame real capture, but that real capture only
exercises 9 of the roughly 20 decoded commands, no Error/NAK messages, no
BACK frame type, and no non-Success response codes (see
\fBdocs/MANUAL.md\fR's LIMITATIONS section and
\fBtests/real_captures/hartip/ATTRIBUTION.md\fR for the full detail); OPC UA
Binary (TCP port 4840, UA-TCP transport / OPC UA Secure Conversation, tried
FIRST of all protocols due to its own strong, collision-free structural
detection gate) is also now decoded -- the 8-byte UA-TCP common header, the
Secure Conversation security/sequence headers, and full field-level
("Tier 1") decode of the connection-lifecycle and discovery services
(Hello/Acknowledge/Error/ReverseHello, OpenSecureChannel, GetEndpoints,
FindServers, CreateSession, ActivateSession -- including, deliberately, the
cleartext Password field of a UserName/Password identity token sent over an
unencrypted channel, flagged as a SECURITY FINDING) -- but OPC UA's own
Variant/DataValue self-describing value encoding is NOT implemented, so
Read/Write/Browse/Call and every subscription service are named and have
their RequestHeader/ResponseHeader decoded only, their own bodies shown as
raw hex; a logical message split across multiple 'C'/'F' OPC UA chunks
(distinct from ordinary TCP-segment reassembly, which IS implemented) is
not reassembled, only a single complete 'F' chunk's body is decoded; there
is no stateful channel/session tracking (a TokenId or AuthenticationToken is
never correlated back to the exchange that issued it); the StatusCode table
is a first-pass ~20 entries out of the OPC Foundation's own ~700, with every
other value still correctly decoding its Good/Uncertain/Bad severity from
the raw hex; and real-capture validation, while genuine (two independent-
stack sessions, 9 of ~15 Tier 1 services, real multi-segment TCP reassembly),
never exercises FindServers, CloseSession, CloseSecureChannel, any Tier 2
service besides Call, a non-Anonymous identity token, a non-'F' chunk, or a
structurally-invalid NodeId (see \fBdocs/MANUAL.md\fR's LIMITATIONS section
and \fBtests/real_captures/opcua/ATTRIBUTION.md\fR for the full detail);
MQTT (v3.1/v3.1.1/v5.0, conventionally TCP port 1883) and, layered on top
of it, Sparkplug B are also now decoded \-\- but MQTT's own structural
detection gate (no fixed magic number or length field) is honestly the
weakest in this codebase, which is why it's dispatched dead last (see
\fB\-\-protocol\fR above); CONNECT/CONNACK/PUBLISH/SUBSCRIBE/SUBACK/
UNSUBSCRIBE/UNSUBACK/PINGREQ/PINGRESP/DISCONNECT/AUTH are all field-decoded
across all three versions, with MQTT5 Properties fully parsed, and
CONNECT's cleartext username/password fields are decoded as a deliberate
SECURITY FINDING (this protocol has no transport encryption of its own);
because SUBSCRIBE/SUBACK/UNSUBSCRIBE's own wire shapes are ambiguous
between MQTT versions, each TCP session's version is tracked from its own
CONNECT packet rather than guessed per-packet where a CONNECT was actually
seen, but a session with no CONNECT in this capture (or a SUBACK alone,
with the weakest evidence of any packet type) still falls back to a
per-packet heuristic; QoS 2 (exactly-once) delivery state, retained
messages, and the Will message's own delivery are not tracked across
packets, only decoded where they appear structurally; Sparkplug B's
protobuf-encoded payloads (NBIRTH/NDEATH/DBIRTH/DDEATH/DDATA/NDATA) are
decoded via a hand-rolled Protocol Buffers reader (no external protobuf
dependency), including the typed Metric value union and nested
Template/DataSet metric types, but the STATE message topic's own JSON
payload is shown only as raw text, not parsed; a real capture (a genuine
Eclipse Paho client's MQTT 3.1 traffic) was found and validated, and it
immediately surfaced a real bug \-\- MQTT 3.1's own session-version
tracking was silently never learned at all, only 3.1.1/5.0 were \-\- found
and fixed; despite a genuine search, no real Sparkplug B capture was
found, so Sparkplug B decoding is validated only against a hand-built
synthetic fixture (see \fBdocs/MANUAL.md\fR's LIMITATIONS section and
\fBtests/real_captures/mqtt/ATTRIBUTION.md\fR for the full detail);
non-IPv4
Ethernet frames and non-TCP
IPv4 payloads (including UDP) are otherwise still just recognized and
NAMED for a small OT-relevant set (ARP, LLDP, ICMP, and the rest) but not further decoded, and
\fBpolicy validate\fR does not yet evaluate any non-TCP traffic (CIP I/O,
PROFINET RT, GOOSE, Sampled Values, EtherCAT, BACnet/IP, and HART-IP's own
UDP traffic included) against a conduit;
Modbus request/response
classification falls back to a payload-shape heuristic only when
authoritative pairing can't be done (no matching request on this session);
S7comm item-level addressing (inside Read Var / Write Var) is fully
confident only for the classic S7ANY syntax; the S7-1200/1500 "symbolic"
syntax (0xB2) gets an EXPERIMENTAL, unverified decode, marked
[EXPERIMENTAL] everywhere it appears;
S7comm-Plus (TCP port 102, shares S7comm's own TPKT/COTP transport,
disambiguated by its own protocol id byte 0x72) is now decoded too, with the
same two-tier posture as MMS/OPC UA: GetMultiVariables, SetMultiVariables,
SetVariable, and DeleteObject (the traffic that dominates real S7comm-Plus
captures) are fully decoded in both directions, including the protocol's own
native symbolic item addressing (a CRC-like hash plus a chain of "LID"
struct/array-member values -- genuinely native, unlike S7comm's own
EXPERIMENTAL 0xB2 reconstruction, though a LID's/CRC's symbolic meaning is
inherently unresolvable without TIA Portal's own project database) and its
self-describing "Value" encoding (20+ datatypes, including recursively
nested STRUCT values); CreateObject, Explore, GetLink, BeginSequence/
EndSequence, Invoke, GetVarSubStreamed, Notification, and Connect are
recognized (named) but not body-decoded, and DataFW1_5 (firmware >= V1.5)
gets header-only decode, since this decoder could not independently confirm
the reference plugin's own byte-accounting for where that variant's body
starts; S7comm-Plus's own above-COTP, trailer-based fragmentation (distinct
from COTP's own EOT bit) is detected and reported but not reassembled; an
array of Struct values is a real but rare shape this decoder deliberately
refuses to decode rather than risk silent misalignment; validated against
two real S7-1511 captures with zero per-item decode errors -- see
\fBdocs/MANUAL.md\fR's PROTOCOL COVERAGE S7comm-Plus section and
\fBtests/real_captures/s7comm/ATTRIBUTION.md\fR's own S7comm-Plus addendum;
IEC 61850 MMS (TCP port 102, shares S7comm's own TPKT/COTP transport) is
also now decoded \-\- the full Session/Presentation/ACSE/MMS layer stack,
including, unlike this tool's own OPC UA decoder, MMS's own self-describing
"Data" value type (14 of 17 alternatives, depth-capped at 32 levels against
a real recursion-assertion denial-of-service bug this decoder's own
validation found in tshark 4.2.2's own MMS dissector) \-\- but only 11 of
MMS's 78 confirmedServices (status, getNameList, identify, read, write,
getVariableAccessAttributes, defineNamedVariableList,
getNamedVariableListAttributes, deleteNamedVariableList,
getDomainAttributes, getCapabilityList) get full field decoding, the rest
are name + invokeID only with the body shown as raw hex; the Session
layer's extended (2-byte) length form is not supported, only the "normal"
one-byte-length-per-parameter form; \fBAddress\fR (non-symbolic variable
addressing) and \fBTypeSpecification\fR are recognized but not decoded;
this decoder assumes the near-universal "1=ACSE, 3=MMS"
presentation-context convention for an ongoing message with no
context-list of its own, corroborated by every real capture checked but an
assumption, not a guarantee; and validation is against three small genuine
real-world captures plus a 224-frame capture this project generated itself
against a real independent stack (\fBmz-automation/libiec61850\fR),
117 of 224 frames recognized, zero parse warnings (see
\fBdocs/MANUAL.md\fR's PROTOCOL COVERAGE MMS section and
\fBtests/real_captures/mms/ATTRIBUTION.md\fR for the full detail, including
two genuine structural-detection-gate bugs this validation pass caught and
fixed);
live capture is an optional,
build-time-detected feature (see LIVE CAPTURE) whose Windows/Npcap path has
not yet been run on a real Windows machine, validated on Linux so far only
against loopback traffic;
\fBpolicy validate\fR's zones are IPv4 CIDR-only and its client/server
(initiator) determination falls back to a port-number heuristic when no
SYN/SYN-ACK was captured for a flow. See the
"LIMITATIONS" and "ROADMAP" sections of
\fBdocs/MANUAL.md\fR in the source tree for the full, current detail and
what's planned next.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Decode a capture as text:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap
.EE
.RE
.PP
Force color into a pager (color is off by default once piped):
.RS
.EX
conduitscope \-\-color decode \-r capture.pcap | less \-R
.EE
.RE
.PP
Get file metadata and a protocol histogram:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope info \-r capture.pcap
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decode as JSON and filter with jq:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-f json | jq '.[] | select(.protocol=="modbus")'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decode IEC 61850 MMS traffic and show every read/write/report value:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol mms \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '.[] | select(.mms_values) | "\e(.src_ip) \-> \e(.dst_ip): \e(.mms_values | join(", "))"'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decode S7comm-Plus (TIA Portal S7-1200/1500) traffic and show every
read/write value:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol s7comm\-plus \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '.[] | select(.s7plus_values) | "\e(.src_ip) \-> \e(.dst_ip): \e(.s7plus_values | join(", "))"'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Summarize IEC 104 ASDUs (type and cause of transmission) from a JSON decode:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol iec104 \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '.[] | select(.iec104_asdu_type) | "\e(.src_ip) \-> \e(.dst_ip): \e(.iec104_asdu_type) (\e(.iec104_cot))"'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Fingerprint EtherNet/IP devices that answered a ListIdentity request:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol enip \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '.[] | select((.enip_command == "ListIdentity") and (.summary | contains("identity:"))) | .src_ip'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Summarize CIP I/O (implicit messaging) traffic by connection:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol enip \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '[.[] | select(.enip_io_connection_id != null)] | group_by(.enip_io_connection_id) |
           .[] | "\e(.[0].enip_io_connection_id): \e(length) datagram(s)"'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Build a quick PROFINET device inventory from DCP exchanges:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol profinet \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '[.[] | select(.profinet_dcp_blocks != null) |
           .profinet_dcp_blocks[] | select(startswith("NameOfStation="))] | unique[]'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Build a quick GOOSE publisher inventory (gocbRef + datSet):
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol goose \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '[.[] | select(.goose_gocb_ref != null) | "\e(.goose_gocb_ref) (\e(.goose_dat_set))"] | unique[]'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Build a quick Sampled Values publisher/stream inventory (svID + smpRate):
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol sv \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '[.[] | select(.sv_id != null) | "\e(.sv_id) smpRate=\e(.sv_smp_rate // "n/a")"] | unique[]'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Spot an EtherCAT Working Counter anomaly (WKC=0, usually a slave that
didn't respond):
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol ethercat \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '.[] | select(.ethercat_first_wkc == 0) |
           "\e(.index): \e(.ethercat_first_cmd_name) adp=\e(.ethercat_first_adp // "n/a")"'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Build a quick BACnet device inventory from I-Am announcements:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol bacnet \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '[.[] | select(.bacnet_service_name == "i\-Am") | "\e(.src_ip): \e(.bacnet_values | join(", "))"] | unique[]'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Build a quick HART-IP device inventory (Unique Identifier responses by address):
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol hartip \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '[.[] | select(.hartip_command_name == "Read Unique Identifier" and .hartip_is_response == true) |
           "\e(.hartip_address): \e(.hartip_values | join(", "))"] | unique[]'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Build an OPC UA endpoint/security-posture inventory -- every distinct
endpoint offered by a server's own GetEndpointsResponse, useful for
spotting an endpoint still accepting SecurityMode "None":
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol opcua \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '[.[] | select(.opcua_service_name == "GetEndpointsResponse") | .opcua_values[] | select(startswith("endpoint["))] | unique[]'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Find every OPC UA ActivateSession request that placed a UserName/Password
credential on the wire in cleartext -- this decoder's own deliberate
"SECURITY FINDING" note, worth flagging on any conduit that should be
running an authenticated, encrypted OPC UA session:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol opcua \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '.[] | select(.notes // [] | any(startswith("SECURITY FINDING"))) |
           "\e(.src_ip):\e(.src_port) \-> \e(.dst_ip):\e(.dst_port): \e(.opcua_values[] | select(startswith("username=") or startswith("password=")))"'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Find MQTT CONNECT packets carrying cleartext credentials -- MQTT's own
Username/Password fields carry no confidentiality of their own, the same
deliberate "SECURITY FINDING" pattern as OPC UA's Identity Token check
above:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol mqtt \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '.[] | select(.mqtt_values // [] | any(startswith("Username=") or startswith("Password="))) |
           "\e(.src_ip):\e(.src_port) \-> \e(.dst_ip):\e(.dst_port): \e(.mqtt_values[] | select(startswith("Username=") or startswith("Password=")))"'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Pull out every decoded Sparkplug B metric across a capture, grouped by
group/edge-node/device:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-\-protocol mqtt \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '.[] | select(.mqtt_is_sparkplug and (.mqtt_sparkplug_metrics // [] | length > 0)) |
           "\e(.mqtt_sparkplug_group_id)/\e(.mqtt_sparkplug_edge_node_id)\e(.mqtt_sparkplug_device_id // "" | if . == "" then "" else "/" + . end) \e(.mqtt_sparkplug_message_type): \e(.mqtt_sparkplug_metrics | join(", "))"'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Surface TCP flows still stuck "buffering" a Modbus/TCP PDU that never
arrives -- the signature of HART-IP's own documented weak-detection-gate
collision (see LIMITATIONS above):
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-r capture.pcap \-f json \e
  | jq \-r '[.[] | select(.summary | test("buffering a (Modbus/TCP PDU|HART\-IP message)")) | .summary] | unique[]'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check a capture against a zone/conduit policy:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope policy validate \-r capture.pcap \-\-policy policy.yaml
.EE
.RE
.PP
List capturable interfaces (requires live\-capture support):
.RS
.EX
conduitscope interfaces
.EE
.RE
.PP
Decode live traffic from eth0 for 60 seconds:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope decode \-i eth0 \-\-filter "port 502 or port 102 or port 5094 or port 4840 or port 1883" \-\-duration 60
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check live traffic against a policy until Ctrl+C:
.RS
.EX
conduitscope policy validate \-i eth0 \-\-policy policy.yaml
.EE
.RE
